.TH PATCH 1
.SH NAME
patch \- apply a unified diff to real files, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B patch
[
.B -nR
]
[
.BI -d\  dir
]
[
.BI -p\  nstrip
]
[
.BI -r\  rejfile
]
[
.I patch ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Patch
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's real
.IR patch (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/patch.c ,
839 lines in the 9front tree), not a behavioral workalike reverse-
engineered from a host tool \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction, and
.B patch.b 's
own header comment for why this one has no live oracle to check
against at all (neither 9front nor plan9port, which does not ship the
same program under this name \(em see BUGS) and is instead verified
against the unified diff format's own specification directly. It lives
under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Patch
reads each
.I patch
argument (or standard input, if none are given) as a unified diff, and
applies every hunk it contains to the files its own
.BR --- / +++
headers name, in place. A patch with hunks for multiple files applies
all of them in one run, grouped and applied in
.RI ( oldpath ", " oldline )
order regardless of the order hunks appear in the patch text.
.PP
Any leading
.B /
characters in a header path are stripped unconditionally \(em even at
the default
.BR -p 0
\(em so header paths are always resolved relative to the current
directory (or
.BR -d ),
never as absolute paths. This is a real property of the original, not
a limitation of this translation; see
.B patch.b 's
own header comment.
.PP
For each target file, the whole new content is assembled in memory and
written to a
.IB new .tmp pid
file next to it, then renamed into place once every hunk for that file
has been resolved \(em so a failure partway through one file's hunks
does not leave it partially modified; see
.B finish()
in
.B patch.b
and BUGS below for the one case (a header pair whose old and new paths
differ) where the original file is not removed even on success.
.SS Options
.TP
.BI -d\  dir
Change to
.I dir
before applying (after parsing the patch itself), so every header path
resolves relative to it.
.TP
.BI -p\  nstrip
Strip
.I nstrip
leading slash-delimited path components from each header path before
the unconditional leading-slash strip above.
.TP
.BI -r\  rejfile
Instead of stopping at the first hunk whose context can't be found,
write it (in the same
.BR --- / +++ /@@
format it came in) to
.I rejfile
and keep going. See BUGS: the exit status does not reflect that some
hunks were rejected.
.TP
.B -n
Dry run: parse and report which files would be touched, without
writing anything.
.TP
.B -R
Apply every hunk backwards \(em swap old and new throughout \(em to
undo a patch that has already been applied.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/patch.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/patch.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR patchtest (1)
.SH BUGS
With
.BR -r ,
a run that rejects one or more hunks and applies the rest still exits
successfully \(em the exit status does not distinguish a fully-applied
patch from a partially-applied one; only the stderr line per rejected
hunk (and the reject file's own contents) says so. This matches the
original.
.PP
plan9port ships a file at
.B src/cmd/scat/patch.c
that shares this program's filename but is a completely different tool
(a star-catalog sky-region utility, "scat" = star catalog) \(em not a
divergent copy of Plan 9's text-diff
.IR patch ,
and not usable as a reference for this translation. See
.B patch.b 's
header comment for the full reasoning on why the unified diff format
itself substitutes for a live oracle here.
.PP
Patch headers are read as Limbo strings (this tree's usual convention
for line-oriented text), not raw bytes the whole way through the way
the original's C buffers are. A target file containing genuinely
invalid UTF-8 (a binary file, or text in another encoding) is not
guaranteed to round-trip byte-exactly through this translation.
.PP
A hunk-set whose old and new paths differ (a patch that renames a
file, e.g.
.BR "git diff -M" 's
output) writes the new content to the new path but never removes the
old one \(em confirmed by reading the original's
.B finish()
line by line, not guessed, and preserved rather than "fixed" since
there is no oracle to confirm which behaviour real Plan 9 patch
intends; see
.IR patchtest (1)'s
own T11.
.PP
A "--- " header line whose filename is empty after stripping leading
whitespace is a genuine infinite loop in the original C (its
.B goto comment
re-examines the same failing line forever) \(em not reproduced here;
this translation raises a parse error instead of hanging the emu VM.
See
.B patch.b 's
header comment.
.PP
.BR close (2)'s
own failure, which the original folds into a combined check alongside
.BR rename ()/ remove (),
has no Limbo equivalent to check (file descriptors are closed by the
garbage collector, not an explicit call that can fail) and is simply
not there.
